//
// @C149 — a function meets the count of 300 variables before it meets the 64 KiB frame cap.
//
// The count is the limit a programmer plans by; the frame cap is a technical one that depends
// on what each variable holds.  So the count has to arrive first for every ordinary variable:
// a function at the limit, 300 variables, fits its frame whatever their type, and only a
// function whose variables are large tuples sees the frame refusal instead.
//
//   * `texts`: 300 `text` parameters and 299 `text` variables, each built by a format string,
//     beside the accumulator — 300 bound variables of the widest plain slot.
//     The answer is the summed length of `a<i>-<i>` for i in 0..299, 2172, and the last
//     parameter's own 4: 2176.
//   * `mixed`: 299 variables each a `(text, integer, float)` tuple.  The answer is the summed
//     `i + len("s<i>")`: 45637.
//   * `wide`: 299 variables each a tuple of 27 integers, 216 bytes apiece — the widest tuple
//     300 variables can all hold: 65536 / 300 is 218 bytes a variable.  The answer is the
//     summed last element, `i + 26`: 52325.  One integer more in each is the frame refusal
//     (`tests/limits.rs`).
//
// Axes it MOVES: what a variable holds (text, a mixed tuple, a wide integer tuple), and
// whether the function also takes 300 parameters.
// Axes HELD FIXED: every variable is live to the end; no struct or vector variable, which
// holds a 12-byte reference and is far inside the bound.
//
// @falsified-at: none — it pins the margin between the two limits, which no earlier build
//   had; a slot that grows, or a frame cap that falls, fails it with the frame refusal.

